    "bitcoin_integration": "Panama has NO enacted comprehensive crypto law and Bitcoin is NOT legal tender. Its constitution 'Monetary Freedom' (Article 262) forbids compulsory legal tender, meaning any currency — including crypto — can circulate by private agreement but the state does not give Bitcoin legal-tender status. Bill No. 697 (2021) passed the National Assembly in April 2022 but was vetoed by President Cortizo in June 2022 over AML/oversight and constitutional concerns. A successor, Bill No. 247 (introduced 2025), would create a FATF-aligned registration/licensing regime for Virtual Asset Service Providers (VASPs), recognize crypto as a valid medium of exchange by agreement, and set up a public registry and suspicious-operation reporting; it remained pending (not enacted) as of 2026. Panama positions itself as a permissive, business-friendly crypto jurisdiction (regional hub for tokenization/real-estate) but without dedicated legislation in force."
